\exfigure{320_q10_1}

Which graph best represents $y=f\big(g(x)\big)$?

solutions.latex:
\exfigure{323_q3_s1}

The square root must be positive (it is also in the denominator), so the domain is $x^2-1>0$.

$\therefore x<-1$ or $x>1$, that is $(-\infty,-1)\cup(1,\infty)$.

\exfigure{339_q7_s1}

Since $f(-x)=-f(x)$, $f$ is odd and its graph has rotational symmetry about the origin.

So the maximum turning point $(2,5)$ maps to a minimum turning point at $(-2,-5)$.

The square root needs $(x+8)(x+2)\ge0$.

\exfigure{373_q12_s1}

Domain: $x\le-8$ or $x\ge-2$.

